.TH SCANNER 1 "2026" "scanner" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
scanner \- read-only intrusion and backdoor scanner for Debian 13
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B scanner
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B scanner
looks for signs of intrusion, backdoors and persistence: reverse and bind shells, hidden or
disguised processes, programs running from /tmp or deleted from disk, new SUID/SGID files,
suspicious systemd units, cron jobs, SSH keys, sshd and PAM settings, programs and file
capabilities where they do not belong, replaced system programs, apt software sources,
suspicious shell history, desktop autostart and session scripts, KDE, GNOME and XFCE
settings, keyboard readers, remote desktop programs, Flatpak and Snap permissions, polkit
rules, downloads and browser add-ons, programs holding saved passwords or cookies open, keys
and tokens that others can read, environment variables that load code, io_uring and unexplained
namespaces, suspicious USB and Thunderbolt devices, packet sniffers, Bluetooth, file sharing
and DNS settings, boot options, AppArmor, failed logins and administrator actions, and pending
updates.
It can also run debsums, chkrootkit, rkhunter and lynis.
.PP
It is strictly read-only: it never changes the system. The only files it writes are the
reports and baselines you ask for, and the automatic baseline in /var/lib/scanner.
.PP
Without options a window opens (when a screen is available) and, if not run as root, asks for
the sudo password to restart with full rights. Any report option gives terminal mode.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-cli
Force terminal mode.
.TP
.B \-\-full
Run everything: all modules, the deep PID scan and all installed external tools.
.TP
.B \-\-json, \-\-html
Write the report as JSON or as a self-contained HTML page.
.TP
.B \-\-summary
Print one line: OK, NOTICE, ATTENTION or ALARM.
.TP
\fB\-o\fR, \fB\-\-output\fR \fIFILE\fR
Write the report to a file.
.TP
.BR \-\-baseline\-diff " [" \fIFILE\fR ]
Show only what changed since a baseline (without
.I FILE
the automatic one, which needs root). The baseline is not modified.
.TP
.BI \-\-modules " LIST"
Comma separated: net_proc, proc_audit, fs_perms, persistence, desktop_session, kernel,
services, auth_logs, integrity, updates. desktop_session is skipped by itself on machines
without a graphical desktop.
.TP
.BI \-\-tools " LIST"
Comma separated: debsums, chkrootkit, rkhunter, lynis, or "all".
.TP
.B \-\-deep\-pid\-scan
Walk the whole PID range to find hidden processes (slower).
.TP
.BI \-\-baseline\-save " FILE"
Save a baseline of SUID/SGID files, listening ports and persistence files.
.TP
.BI \-\-baseline\-compare " FILE"
Compare with a saved baseline. Without it the automatic baseline is used.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-auto\-baseline
Do not save or use the automatic baseline.
.TP
\fB\-c\fR, \fB\-\-config\fR \fIFILE\fR
Whitelist in TOML or JSON.
.TP
.B \-\-min\-severity \fILEVEL\fR
Only show findings from low, medium, high or critical upwards.
.TP
.B \-\-root \fIDIR\fR
Scan another root file system, for example a mounted disk.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-sudo
Do not ask for the sudo password.
.TP
.BI \-\-update\-config " [FILE]"
Create the whitelist, or add this version's new default entries to it (default
/etc/scanner/whitelist.toml). Own entries and comments are kept; removed entries stay removed.
.TP
.B \-\-self\-test
Run the safe self-test in a temporary sandbox.
.TP
.B \-\-print\-config
Print the default whitelist as TOML.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-tools
Show which external tools are installed.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 no high or critical findings, 1 at least one high, 2 at least one critical, 3 error.
.SH FILES
.TP
/etc/scanner/whitelist.toml
System whitelist. Else ~/.config/scanner/whitelist.toml.
.TP
/var/lib/scanner/baseline.json
Automatic baseline (root only).
.TP
/var/log/scanner/
Reports from the nightly scanner\-diff.timer.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR debsums (1),
.BR chkrootkit (1),
.BR rkhunter (8),
.BR lynis (8),
/usr/share/doc/scanner/README.md
.SH AUTHOR
https://github.com/mobado/scanner
